       All  options  can  be abbreviated to their shortest unique prefix.  You
       may use two hyphens instead of one to designate an option.  You may use
       either  white  space  or  an equals sign between an option name and its
       value.



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmddiitthheerr reads a Netpbm image as input, and applies dithering to it to
       reduce the number of colors used down to the specified number of shades
       for each primary.  It produces a PPM image as output.

       The default number of shades is 5 red, 9 green, and 5 blue, for a total
       of  225  colors.   To convert the image to a binary rgb format suitable
       for primitive color printers, use --rreedd==22 --ggrreeeenn==22 --bblluuee==22.

       You can do another kind of dither -- Floyd-Steinberg --  with  ppnnmmqquuaanntt
       or ppnnmmrreemmaapp.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppppmmddiitthheerr recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --ddiimm _p_o_w_e_r
               The  size  of  the dithering matrix.  The dithering matrix is a
              square whose dimension is a power of 2.  _p_o_w_e_r is that power  of
              2.  The default is 4, for a 16 by 16 matrix.


       --rreedd=_n The number of red shades to be used, including black; minimum of
              2.

              Default is 5.


       --ggrreeeenn _n
              The number of green shades to be used, including black;  minimum
              of 2.

              Default is 9.


       --bblluuee _n
              The  number  of blue shades to be used, including black; minimum
              of 2.

              Default is 5.



